AU  - Alan M. Dietzek
AU  - Rima Ahmad
AU  - Michele Richard
JO  - Elsevier eBooks
PY  - 2019
SP  - 215
EP  - 231
DO  - 10.1016/b978-0-323-51139-1.00006-1
AB  - Abstract In 1999 the first-generation radiofrequency (RF) device, known as the Closure Procedure, was introduced. In this early device iteration, bipolar electrodes mounted on the end of a catheter delivered radiofrequency energy to the inner wall of the vein. The next generation device, the ClosureFAST RF ablation catheter, eliminated the need for a slow catheter pullback by implementing stationary segmental treatment at 120°C. The morphologic and hemodynamic outcomes following RF ablation have been described in detailed ultrasound studies by Pichot in which the pathologic sequelae of a treated vein are reflected by its sonographic progression. Occluded veins were initially hypoechogenic compared with the surrounding tissue and gradually evolved into hyperechogenic and eventually isoechogenic presentations, indicating a final healing process. Incomplete or failed vein ablation, either partial or total, constituting anatomic failure, was first described with RF technology. Patients reported in the registry obtained clinical benefit with symptom improvement despite evidence of anatomic failure. Four types of SFJ morphologies have been identified after RF ablation and are described in this chapter. There are 7 RCTs in 9 papers comparing RFA with surgery and almost all of them conclude that after radiofrequency ablation there was less postoperative pain, faster recovery, and earlier return to work and normal activities as well as higher patient satisfaction. Five RCTs compared RFA with EVLA and demonstrated less bruising and less pain with segmental RF ablation.
