AB  - Chronic venous diseases (CVD) include a spectrum of clinical findings ranging from spider telangiectasias and varicose veins to debilitating venous ulceration. Varicose veins without skin changes, the focus of this chapter, are present in about 20% of the general population. The population-based costs in the United States for treatment of chronic venous insufficiency and venous ulcer care have been estimated to exceed one billion dollars a year. Saphenectomy, known as surgical high ligation and stripping, has long been the standard of care for the treatment of CVD. In the United States, saphenectomy is rapidly becoming senescent because studies have shown that endovenous thermal ablation is safe and effective with faster recovery and better cosmesis. When compared with stripping procedures, thermal ablation (EVL and RF) procedures produce significantly less postoperative pain, less postoperative analgesia requirements, and faster recovery. Advantages also included fewer adverse events. Bipolar radiofrequency ablation (85□°C) and chemical ablation are equally effective at achieving a successful outcome (77–84% success), but that endovenous laser ablation was slightly more effective (94% success). A second-generation RF device (120□°C) awaits long-term results. Ambulatory phlebectomy is cost-effective, durable, and gives superb cosmetic results. CHIVA, ASVAL, and compression are also discussed.
