TY  - JOUR
TI  - WHAT’S NEW IN SURGERY What’s New in Vascular Surgery
AU  - William H. Pearce
PY  - 2003
AB  - This year marked the 50th anniversary of the American Association for Vascular Surgery, formerly the International Society for Cardiovascular Surgery North American Chapter and the 53rd meeting of the Society for Vascular Surgery. Vascular surgery continues to evolve into a mature specialty. But recent issues regarding surgical lifestyle have challenged both general surgery and vascular surgery. The paper by Bland and Issacs highlighted the problems with the decreasing number of medical students choosing surgical careers. By their estimates, in 2005, only 76% of United States general surgery slots will be filled by United States students. In a review of the vascular surgical training programs, there has been a slight decline in the number of applicants per position (1.9 to 1.3). Vascular surgical training programs are now incorporating endovascular techniques into their training programs and as a result, many programs are now 2 years in duration. So the challenge before the vascular surgical community is to shorten training programs to attract high-quality candidates, while ensuring that all graduates have considerable endovascular training. In December 2002, the current editors of The Journal of Vascular Surgery, Dr Wayne Johnston and Dr Robert Rutherford, will complete their 6-year term. Dr Jack Cronenwett of Dartmouth and Dr James Seeger of the University of Southern Florida will replace them as the new editors of the Journal beginning January 2003. Although there have been many new developments in the past year in the understanding of vascular biology and surgical and endovascular treatment of vascular disease, there were two advances that will change the practice of vascular surgery. The important advances are in our management of small abdominal aortic aneurysms and the use of stents to treat lower extremity arterial disease. The results of the Aneurysm Detection and Management (ADAM) trial and the United Kingdom (UK) Small Aneurysm Trial appeared in the May issue of the New England Journal of Medicine. These articles describe large randomized, prospective studies that compared immediate repair of small abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAAs) with surveillance. Although there is a slight difference in methodology, the conclusions were similar. Small aneurysms (those less than 5.5 cm) can be safely managed by frequent surveillance (every 6 months). Survival is not improved in either study by early elective repair. But many of the patients crossed over into the surgically treated arm. In the ADAM trial the operative mortality (within 30 days) was 1.8%. This low mortality figure reflects continual improvements in the perioperative care of vascular surgical patients. Operative mortality in the UK study was substantially higher (6.2%) before 1998 and dropped to 4.3% in recent years. Of note, the British trial included 17% women. The authors questioned whether the threshold of 5.5-cm diameter might be too high in female patients who have small aortas and a greater propensity for rupture. Interestingly the UK study found that early surgery was associated with a statistically improved longterm survival (9-year study): 53% in the surgery arm versus 45% with surveillance. This improvement in longterm mortality can be associated with lifestyle changes, which were not evaluated in this study. Perhaps one of the most important basic science discoveries made in the past year has been the use of bone marrow cells to produce endothelial cell progenitor cells to treat patients with ischemic heart or peripheral vascular disease. Kawamoto described a set of animal experiments at the recent Research Initiatives Program in BeReceived November 21, 2002; Accepted November 21, 2002. From the Division of Vascular Surgery, Northwestern Memorial Hospital, and Northwestern University, The Feinberg School of Medicine, Chicago, IL. Correspondence address: William H Pearce, MD, 201 E Huron, #10-105, Chicago, IL 60611.
