AU  - Matthew Tan
AU  - Safa Salim
AU  - Sarah Onida
AU  - Alun H. Davies
JO  - Journal of Vascular Surgery Venous and Lymphatic Disorders
PY  - 2020
VL  - 9
IS  - 1
SP  - 264
EP  - 274
DO  - 10.1016/j.jvsv.2020.07.011
AN  - 32791308
AB  - BACKGROUND\nCompression following sclerotherapy is commonly used, although the evidence base for this is unclear. This study aims to summarise and assess the evidence for compression therapy post-sclerotherapy to inform clinical practice.\n\n\nMETHODS\nA systematic review was performed according to PRISMA guidelines via Medline and EMBASE databases (1946 to 31st December 2019) by two reviewers. Full-text, English language studies comparing compression type and/or duration in adult chronic venous disease patients undergoing liquid or foam sclerotherapy were included.\n\n\nRESULTS\nNine studies were identified: five employing liquid sclerotherapy, three foam sclerotherapy and one using both. Studies had short follow-up periods (6 to 24 weeks) and reported on clinical outcomes, quality of life, side effects and complications. In C1 patients undergoing liquid sclerotherapy, any duration of stocking use significantly decreased telangiectasia and reticular vein number and size compared to no compression. No significant difference in clinical symptoms or quality of life was seen when comparing compression duration after liquid or foam sclerotherapy in tributary or truncal veins in C2-6 patients. Greater superficial vein resolution was seen with stockings compared to bandages in C2 patients undergoing liquid sclerotherapy to tributary veins. A comparison of stockings versus bandaging revealed differing thrombophlebitis rates but no significant difference in pigmentation. In C2-6 patients undergoing foam sclerotherapy, use of 35mmHg stockings significantly improved post-treatment symptoms compared to 23mmHg stockings. This review was limited by heterogeneity of outcome measurements and the variety of comparisons between compression types and durations.\n\n\nCONCLUSIONS\nPost-sclerotherapy compression may have beneficial clinical outcomes at short-term follow-up; evidence is lacking regarding its type, class, length and duration. Further trials are required to guide the optimal management of post-sclerotherapy patients.
