AB  - Background: Varicose vein disease is a common disease in nearly 37.25% of Indian population affecting quality of life. Endovenous thermal ablation has been the first-line treatment for superficial venous reflux. A new technique, available now, is nonthermal, nontumescent vein sealing system (VenaSeal™), which comprises endovenous delivery of cyanoacrylate tissue adhesive into the vein causing obliteration of lumen and has shown better results in terms of safety and efficacy. Being a new modality, only a handful of institutes across India perform this procedure and there is a scarcity of Indian data on its outcomes. Aims and Objectives: To evaluate the outcomes of VenaSeal™ glue ablation in terms of vein occlusion rates and improvement in VCSS score. Materials and Methods: The Indian VenaSeal Experience Study was carried out a year back, which was a single-center prospective study of 50 patients over 1 year and 4 months with 3 and 6 months of follow-up, and we aimed to evaluate the outcomes of VenaSeal™ glue ablation in terms of vein occlusion rates and improvement in venous clinical severity score (VCSS). This is an extension of that study with 200 patients (378 limbs) with similar parameters for outcome evaluation. Results: The study showed the mean age of study group to be 48.6 years and majority (87%) had an early varicose vein (C2/C3) disease. The average length of vein treated per limb was 62.6 cm and the average glue used per limb was 3.05 ml. All patients showed improvement in VCSS score averaging 2.3 ± 1.6 ( P &lt; 0.0001, n = 378) with target vein occlusion rate of 98% at 6 months ( n = 182), 97% at 12 months ( n = 126), and 95% at 2 years ( n = 98). Conclusion: VenaSeal™ glue ablation procedure is a promising and effective modality for the treatment of varicose veins with excellent treatment success rates and maximum patient acceptability. However, complications such as severe phlebitis and anaphylaxis have been reported. It is a feasible option under local anesthesia, does not require tumescence, and hence an efficient outpatient department-based procedure.
