AB  - OBJECTIVES\nTo assess the mid-term efficacy and safety of thermal and non-thermal endovenous ablation in the treatment of lower limb superficial venous insufficiency.\n\n\nMETHOD\nWe performed a systematic review according to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-analyses (PRISMA) statement and a Bayesian network meta-analysis. Primary endpoints were great saphenous vein (GSV) closure and venous clinical severity score (VCSS) score improvement. A meta-regression employing GSV diameter as a covariate was undertaken for the two primary endpoints.\n\n\nRESULTS\nFourteen studies and 4177 patients with a mean follow-up of 25.7 months were included. Radiofrequency ablation (RFA), odds ratio (OR) 3.99 (95%CI:1.82, 10.53), cyanoacrylate ablation (CAC), OR 3.09 (95%CI:1.35, 8.37) and endovenous laser ablation (EVLA), OR 2.72 (95%CI:1.23, 7.38) displayed increased odds for GSV closure compared to mechanochemical ablation (MOCA). MOCA inferiority compared to RFA, mean difference (MD) 0.96 (95%CI:0.71, 1.20), EVLA, MD 0.94 (95%CI:0.61, 1.24) and CAC MD 0.89 (95%CI:0.65, 1.15) was also depicted regarding VCSS improvement. EVLA showcased an increased risk for post-operative paresthesia compared to MOCA, risk ratio (RR) 9.61 (95%CI:2.32, 62.29), CAC RR 7.90 (95%CI:2.44, 38.16) and RFA 6.96 (95%CI:2.31, 28.04). Although the overall analysis identified non-statistically significant differences concerning the Aberdeen varicose vein questionnaire (AVVQ) score improvement, thrombophlebitis, ecchymosis and pain, further investigation portrayed an increase pain profile for EVLA 1470nm compared to RFA MD 3.22 (95%CI: 0.93, 5.47) and CAC MD 3.04 (95%CI: 1.05, 4.97). Sensitivity analysis displayed a persistent underperformance of MOCA compared to RFA OR 4.33 (95%CI:1.15, 55.54) regarding GSV closure and both RFA MD 0.99 (95%CI:0.22, 1.77) and CAC MD 0.84 (95%CI: 0.08, 1.65) regarding VCCS improvement. Whereas no regression model managed to reach statistical significance, the GSV closure regression model revealed a tendency for both CAC and MOCA to considerably decrease their efficacy for larger GSV diameters compared to RFA and EVLA.\n\n\nCONCLUSION\nAlbeit our analysis produces skepticism about the efficacy of MOCA in the mid-term period regarding VCSS improvement and GSV closure rates, CAC indicated equivalent results compared to both RFA and EVLA. Additionally, CAC displayed a decreased risk for post-procedural paresthesia and pigmentation-induration compared to EVLA, while both RFA and CAC showcased an improved pain profile compared to EVLA 1470nm. The potential underperformance of NTNT modalities in ablating large GSV necessitates further research.
